    "title": "AKI likely pre-renal, not TDF toxicity",
    "message": "Guidelines state that in patients on TDF admitted to hospital, intercurrent illnesses with dehydration or sepsis may cause renal deterioration and creatinine should be checked. The patient has severe gastroenteritis with 5 days of vomiting and diarrhoea, hypotension (90/60), tachycardia, and physical signs of dehydration. The urea-to-creatinine ratio is 24:1, strongly suggesting pre-renal AKI. Urinalysis shows no protein, which argues against TDF-induced tubular toxicity. Two family members share the illness, supporting infectious gastroenteritis. TDF can cause proximal tubular injury and Fanconi syndrome, but the acute presentation with a high pre-renal ratio and dehydration makes gastroenteritis-induced AKI far more likely. Permanent TDF discontinuation may be unnecessary if renal function recovers with rehydration.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Rehydrate and recheck creatinine before deciding to stop TDF permanently. If creatinine normalises, TDF may be continued with ongoing renal monitoring.",
